{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Go deeper</p>
  <h1>Routes and tests</h1>
  <p class="lede">A request's path is a string, and Roc's <code>match</code> picks the page.
  A page is a value until it is sent, so a test compares it whole.</p>
</header>

<section>
  <p>These are not pages yet:
  <a class="pill" href="/greet/Ada">/greet/Ada</a>
  <a class="pill" href="/greet/Grace">/greet/Grace</a></p>
</section>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><p>In <code>main.roc</code>, find <code>page</code>. Where it says <em>a route of your own
    goes here</em>, add:</p>
    <pre><code>["", "greet", name] =&gt; Ok(Rocstache.html(Greeting.template({ frame: frame("/routes"), name })))</code></pre>
    <p>Follow the links again. The pattern binds <code>name</code> to that part of the path.</p></li>
    <li><p>At the bottom of <code>main.roc</code>, test it:</p>
    <pre><code>expect page("/greet/Ada") == Ok(Rocstache.html(Greeting.template({ frame: frame("/routes"), name: "Ada" })))</code></pre>
    <p>In a second terminal: <code>roc test main.roc</code>. Change <code>"Ada"</code> on one side
    and watch it fail, naming both values.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How a request flows</h2>
  <ol class="flow">
    <li><code>init!</code> runs once, at start: it opens the database and returns the app's
    <code>Context</code>, shared by every request.</li>
    <li><code>respond!</code> runs for each request, on its own lightweight thread; the routes
    that read a file or the database are matched there.</li>
    <li>Everything else goes to <code>page</code>, a plain function: a path in, a response out.
    It renders nothing and reads nothing, so <code>roc test</code> can call it.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <code>!</code> marks a function with effects; Roc tracks them in the types. A test can
  also reach inside a response, as the one in <code>main.roc</code> does:</p>
  <pre><code>expect
	match page("/") {
		Ok({ status: 200, body: Html({ template: Start(start_page), .. }), .. }) =&gt; start_page.hello == hello
		_ =&gt; False
	}</code></pre>
  <p>The HTML itself is roux's to get right: it tests its renderer against an oracle over
  thousands of generated templates. Your tests are about your app.</p>
</section>
